v32opt known issues -- verified by audit + repro, Sep 15 2026
(updated; supersedes earlier notes. "RMW" = read-modify-write to memory,
 e.g. "IADD [R1+0], 5")

CONFIRMED MISCOMPILES (each reproduced with a minimal input):

-fpeephole-immediate-prop
  Pattern 2 (MOV + ALU constant folding) scans past reads of the
  register and past JT/JF branches. "MOV R1,10 / MOV R2,R1 / IADD R1,5"
  folds to "MOV R1,15 / MOV R2,R1" (R2 now sees 15, not 10); folding
  across a JT also changes the value seen on the taken path.

-fpeephole-forwarding
  Rule 1 treats only "MOV [mem],x" as a memory store. An intervening
  RMW to the same address ("IADD [R1+0],5") or an aliasing store via a
  different base register holding the same address is not seen, so the
  later load is wrongly replaced with a register move. MOVS/SETS also
  unmodeled. (Likely cause of the old "premature HLT" reports on real
  v32lua output, which emits RMW loop counters like "IADD [BP-2],1".)

-fpeephole-dead-stores
  Case 2 counts ANY instruction with the same indirect destination as
  an "exact overwrite". An RMW ("IADD [BP-4],1") READS the slot too, so
  the feeding store is wrongly deleted. MOVS/SETS also unmodeled.

-fpeephole-jmp-chain
  Retargeting a jump past a label is fine, but the pass also DELETES
  the intermediate JMP even when other branches still target that
  label; they now fall through into whatever followed it. Also burns
  2 --trigger-max slots for one transform (bisection off-by-one).

-fpeephole-shifts
  "SHL r,N; SHL r,-N" removal is not identity: shifted-out bits are
  gone, so (x<<N)>>N != x. "SHL r,r" (dst==src register) is removed as
  a no-op but is a shift by r's own value.

-fpeephole-pairs
  "NOT r; NOT r" removal is only identity for boolean r; for any other
  value it computes CIB(r) (0/1), not r. XOR self-cancel pair compares
  only the base register of indirect destinations, not the offset
  ("XOR [R1],R2; XOR [R1+4],R2" is deleted).

-fomit-frame-pointers
  [BP-N] accesses are treated as "not using BP" (offset>=0 test), so
  functions whose locals live at [BP-N] with no other BP/SP use get
  their frame stripped and their locals redirected into the caller's
  frame. (Unit test scenario 3/4 encodes this wrong expectation.)

-fcse
  Only the destination register is tracked between the two matching
  expressions. If a SOURCE register (or the memory a source operand
  points at) is modified in between, the second computation is still
  replaced by a move of the stale first result.

-finline (edge cases)
  Function-end detection stops only at RET / "MOV SP,BP": a body that
  ends in HLT (noreturn helpers) swallows the following function (and
  any data directives) into the candidate body. "MOV BP,x" writes are
  not rejected. [BP+N]->[SP+N-2] rewrite assumes args were pushed
  immediately before the CALL (no intervening SP moves are verified).

DEAD CODE / MISSED OPTIMIZATIONS:

-fpeephole-immediates, -fpeephole-reduce
  All float branches are unreachable: is_numeric_immediate() rejects
  every is_float operand, so FADD/FSUB combining and FMUL/FDIV by
  0.0/1.0 never fire. (If re-enabled, note float cancellation
  "(x+a)-a == x" is NOT exact in float32 -- do not remove such pairs.)

--dot with any node-removing pass (e.g. -O2)
  CFG was built before Phase-3 cleanup; export walks freed nodes
  (use-after-free; currently "works" only by allocator luck).

general
  CALL/RET move SP (hardware return-address push/pop) but
  modifies_register() doesn't model it; SP-relative forwarding across
  a CALL is theoretically wrong (rare in compiler output).
  main() frees only the list head (whole-program leak; harmless for
  output correctness).

UNIT TESTS (make -C testing), current state:
  pass: omit-frame-pointers, peephole-algebra, peephole-compiler-myopia,
        peephole-dead-stores, peephole-immediate-prop, peephole-loads,
        peephole-movs, primes, promote-loops
        (earlier notes that movs/loads/dead-stores/forwarding hang or
        refuse to run are stale -- they run; forwarding's real problems
        are the RMW/alias holes above)
  fail (code bugs): cse (source-operand tracking), shifts + pairs
        (fixtures encode the unsound SHL-cancel / SHL r,r / NOT-NOT
        expectations -- fixing the pass will "break" these fixtures),
        immediates + reduce (float dead code)
  fail (stale/misbuilt fixtures): peephole-forwarding (expects the old
        immediate-propagation behavior now delegated to
        peephole-immediate-prop), peephole-jumps (scenarios not
        separated by labels, so one scenario's dead zone legitimately
        eats the next; KEEP marks are over-strict), peephole-jmp-chain
        (expects full multi-hop collapse), inline (cascaded inlining
        after a callee becomes leaf), constant-folding (fixture uses
        the reserved word "skip" as a label -- never assembles)
